  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1155 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nSunday the pattern continues to shift as a large scale weather\nsystem deepens over western CONUS. Still, the high pressure system\nwill remain the main weather feature across the southern plains.\nThe interaction between the two system will most likely lead to an\nincrease in the pressure gradient. This in turn will cause the\nwinds to increase across the panhandles on Sunday. With the\ngenerally hot and dry conditions of the high pressure system the\nincrease in the winds will bring elevated fire weather conditions\nback to the panhandles. Towards the later portions of Sunday a\nsmall weather system can brush the western panhandles bringing a\nlow chance for rain showers and thunderstorms. The moisture will\nbe a limiting factor so even if a storm manages to form it will\nnot lead to much if any meaningful rainfall. \n\nThe large scale weather system pushes into the southern panhandles\nstarting next work week. This will bring a shift in the weather\npattern with a more southwesterly flow setting up across the\nsouthern plains. This flow will draw in moisture into the\npanhandles while the passage of the weather system will bring\ninstability and dynamics. This opens the panhandles up to a medium\nchance for rain showers and thunderstorms each day of next work\nweek. This will allow the panhandles to have a shot at meaningful\nrainfall for all of next work week. Still, since the most likely\nmode of rainfall will be showers there will be winners and loser\nwhen it comes to how much rain occurs for any given spot. Another\nbenefit of this more active period of weather is that the \ntemperatures will most likely cool off.",
